Nick Szabo wrote:

>Also, some folks have complained that Extropianism emphasizes the
>far future too much.  But in fact the original contribution of 
>Extropianism has to do with the far future and its consequences
>on our prospects starting in the here and now.  Extropianism without
>these crucial insights about the far future would not be Extropianism,
>it would be just another dead end fad popularization of philosophy.  I 
>do agree that transforming our current lives, in light of those insights
>about the far future, should recieve more emphasis, but we cannot
>abandon exploration of the shape of that far future without 
>abandoning Extropianism.

I agree completely. However, there are some problems with emphasizing the
far future and the really dramatic consequences of our ideas. It gives us
more direction and scope, and doubtless attracts many to extropianism (at
least I was attracted mainly by these visions, and I think most others
were too). But at the same time these ideas tend to grab attention away
from more practical aspects, since the human mind tends to focus on high
pay-offs, even if they are remote or improbably (just look at how many 
participate in lotteries). 

What we need is to both be able to have grand visions and still remain 
practical. This is a hard balancing act, as its easy to get bogged down in 
details and practical concerns on one hand, and equally easy to just 
speculate and imagine things on the other hand. 

