Subject: [#94-11-450] GENE: Resurrecting the dodo?

> Anders Sandberg wrote:
>
> Nancy Lebovitz responded:
> >As I recall, one of the reasons that dodos became extinct was that
> >people ate them--maybe dodos taste good.
> 
> This must be studied. Although I doubt they would become a major food
> product (there are many more established birds, but the dodo may be a
> novelty food). But who knows? 
 
Unlikely -- the nickname given to the Dodo by Dutch settlers was "nauseous
bird", because it tasted so bad.  Nevertheless, the settlers and the species
they imported (especially hogs) were responsible for the dodo's extinction.
So, though the dodo is often cited as an example of evolutionary failure,
this isn't really fair:  it would probably still be around, had not predators
evolution had not been prepared it for been introduced to its environment.
The same is true of lots of other extinct species; that such species are
not as adaptable as, say, humans are is not good grounds, I think, for
classifying them as "useless" (in Geoff Dale's word).

