From: Webb_S <WebbS@oplc.psb.bls.gov>
Date: Mon, 28 Nov 94 10:27:00 EST
Subject: [#94-11-451] GENE: Resurrecting the dodo?

Michael Price writes:

>> Ah, but dinosaurs _are_ birds! (that is, if you favor BCF theory,
>> as I do.  Otherwise, birds are dinosaurs.)
>
>Sounds like there's an interesting story here.  Could you explain the
>difference?  What's BCF?

Birds came first or BCF is a theory proposed by George Olshevsky as an 
alternative to what he calls the BADD theory (birds are dinosaurs 
descendants) of Ostrom et al.  Problems with BADD that BCF seeks to answer 
include:

 -  The lack of birdlike dinosaurs before Archaeopteryx, supposedly the first 
true bird.

 -  The fact that birds were much smaller than most dinosaurs, which violates 
Cope's rule of evolution (small forms evolve from larger forms, not 
vice-versa).

 -  The wing problem:  no birdlike dinosaur had arms and shoulders which 
would have been of any use in flying.  Flight requires such a large number 
of changes to the basic therapod body that it's difficult to see what use 
small changes in the evolution of wings would have been to ground-dwelling 
dinos.

Olshevsky proposes that all dinosaurs and modern birds evolved from small, 
tree-dwelling "dino-birds" like Cosesaurus, Megalancosaurus, and 
Longisquama.    BCF is detailed in an article in the June '94 _Omni_, and I 
think it goes a long way toward sorting out the previously confusing picture 
of archosaur evolution.

If birds are descended from dinosaurs, it is appropriate to say that birds 
_are_ dinosaurs, since T. Rex is more closely related to modern birds than 
to any other dinosaur most people can name.  If BCF is correct, the 
dino-bird ancestors of Archaeopteryx branched away from the central lineage 
before the ancestors of advanced therapods such as T Rex.  Dinosaurs, 
primitive birds, and modern birds would be grouped together under the 
heading "birds," and thus one could say that dinosaurs were birds.


