From: fcp@nuance.com (Craig Presson)
Date: Mon, 28 Nov 1994 10:08:55 -0600
Subject: [#94-11-454] COMPUTERS: the persistence of C

At 07:45 PM 11/27/94, extropians@extropy.org wrote:
>Craig Presson: 
>> The market for programming close to the hardware has shrunk enormously
>> in the last 10 years as the number of different hardware platforms has
>> decreased.
>
>This is simply wrong.  The number of new platforms has grown with the
>RISC revolution, and we have games programming on top of that -- an
>applications area where programming close to the hardware is especially
>highly prized.  Unix porting (read: rewriting most of the kernel
>to get something that does databases at least halfway efficiently)
>is still just about as big as it ever was,[...]

15 years ago, there were more computer architectures than there
were hardware suppliers, and each company had to have brigades of
low-level programmers, of which I used to lead one. The CISC/RISC
wars have shaken out to a considerable extent, to where only a
handful (and eventually 2 or 3: the descendants of Pentium, PowerRISC,
and Alpha) of different chips will have any market share at all.
Even the number of Unix variants in any kind of wide use has decreased,
although never quite to the promised value of 1.00 :-)

I don't see this trend reversing, people *want* compatibility, and the
ROI on new processor architectures will not be sufficient. 

I think even specialized niches like games programming will move to
some kind of VHLOOL, as processors and cycles will not be the scarce
commodity any more. They're not today, for end-user apps, as individual
desktops carry 3 to 20 MIPS and people find other parts of the system
(network, video, disk) to complain about.

I'm not complaining about C, I'd just as soon keep using it, but the
proportion of projects where performance is a bleeding edge as a
fraction of all new developments should continue to decrease -- if
we're doing our job, and not just muddying the waters to keep from
having to learn new stuff.

The way I could be way wrong is if programmers continue to
find new ways to waste resources, so that processors never catch up
and bumming cycles never reaches diminishing returns. I'd like to 
call that unlikely, but I think there are indications both ways.



 
