From: pavel@PARK.BU.EDU (Paul Cisek)
Date: Mon, 28 Nov 1994 12:47:49 -0500
Subject: [#94-11-458] COMP: Computation with DNA

Here's an interesting piece of news:

> [forwards removed]
>
>ARTICLE TIME AND DATE: NYT-11-21-94 2257EST .
>01:57 EST NOVEMBER 22, 1994
>
>THE SCIENCE TIMES: SCIENTIST USES DNA TO SOLVE MATH PROBLEMS
>
>By GINA KOLATA
>c.1994 N.Y. Times News Service
>
>   In a bold experiment that is provoking investigators to reconsider what a
>computer is and what it means to compute, a researcher has used DNA, the
>genetic material, as a sort of personal computer.
>
>   Exploiting the extraordinary efficiency and speed of biological reactions,
>he translated a difficult mathematical problem into the language of molecular
>biology and solved it by carrying out a reaction in one-fiftieth of a teaspoon
>of solution in a test tube.
>
>   The experiment's designer, Dr. Leonard Adleman of the University of Southern
>California in Los Angeles, discovered a way to use DNA to solve a problem that
>involves finding the shortest path linking seven cities. This is one of a class
>of famously hard mathematical problems that have no simple solutions and can,
>in general, only be solved by trying out all possibilities. Adleman described
>his experiment in the Nov. 11 issue of the journal Science.
>
>   Molecular computers, Adleman said, are breathtakingly fast and efficient,
>and they have unheard of storage capacities. He said that molecular computers
>can perform more than a trillion operations per second, which makes them a
>thousand times as fast as the fastest supercomputer. They are a billion times
>as energy efficient as conventional computers. And they can store information
>in a trillionth of the space required by ordinary computers.
>
>   Whether or not such molecular computing will prove practical, the experiment
>is tantalizing, computer scientists said.
>
>   ``It's a very intriguing idea,'' said Dr. Ronald L. Graham, adjunct director
>of information science at AT&T. Bell Laboratories in Murray Hill, N.J. ``It's
>more than just cute. It makes you think in a different direction.''
>
>   Dr. David Jefferson, a computer scientist at the University of California at
>Los Angeles, said: ``I am incredibly impressed. This is the first attempt to my
>knowledge of an undeniable computational problem solved by molecular
>methods. It shows a deeper unity between computational science and life science
>than was ever demonstrated before. I think it's fantastic.''
>
>   Dr. David Gifford, a computer science professor at the Massachusetts
>Institute of Technology, said: ``It's quite exciting. It's a completely new way
>to think about computing. Our minds are prejudiced to think about computing in
>terms of computers we build ourselves. But it's important to free our minds to
>think about how computing might occur naturally.''
>
>   In addition, Gifford said, the experiment at least raises the question of
>whether biological systems actually compute in a more deterministic fashion
>than had been imagined. In evolution, for example, it is supposed that changes
>in DNA occur at random and lead, by the process of natural selection, to new
>organisms. ``But there is a question of whether random events can be guided
>along productive paths,'' Gifford said.
>
>   Adleman, he added, used DNA segments to help direct a search process,
>finding the best solution to his problem from trillions of wrong
>solutions. ``It's conceivable that there are similar mechanisms at work in
>evolution,'' Gifford said.
>
>   Jefferson said that another way to look at the evolution question is to ask
>whether there are some computational problems that are best handled by
>molecular computers and others that are not. Then, he said, scientists can ask
>whether evolution favors mechanisms that are easy to handle with molecular
>computations?
>
>   Besides its provocative implications, Jefferson said, the new experiment is
>the first example of true nanotechnology, or technology on an atomic scale.
>
>   ``We certainly know in general that some form of molecular computation will
>be feasible in the coming decades,'' said Dr. Ralph Merkle, a computer
>scientist who studies nanotechnology at Xerox's Palo Alto Research Center in
>California. The DNA experiment ``illustrates that we're in an era now where
>unexpected advances are not only possible but probable,'' he said. ``It's quite
>possible that the route taken will be quite surprising.''
>
>   Adleman said his DNA experiment grew out of a year he spent in a molecular
>biology laboratory satisfying his curiosity about the burgeoning field of
>molecular genetics. ``It was always in my mind that molecular biology has
>computational aspects to it,'' he said. ``A computer scientist quickly learns
>that any sort of system where you can have organized, predictable actions or
>reactions can be parlayed into a computational system.''
>
>   He explained that the heart of ordinary computers consists of chips, and
>chips perform very simple operations involving 0's and 1's. ``If you look at
>the basic operations in a computer, you find them very unimpressive,'' Adleman
>said. ``But you can parlay these simple operations into much more complicated
>things.''
>
>   But it is one thing to get a notion that biological molecules might be
>exploited to do computations and another to figure out how to do it.  Adleman
>said he thought for six months before figuring out a simple experiment that
>would use DNA as a computer.
>
>   The problem, finding the shortest possible path to connect seven cities when
>he specified the first and the last city in the sequence, can only be solved in
>general by finding and trying out every possible path to see which is shortest.
>
>   For example, if a traveler wanted to start in Baltimore, travel to nine
>other cities and end the trip in Atlanta, what would be the shortest path he
>could take and pass through each city once?
>
>   Graham said that although it is not hard for modern computers to do a
>problem involving seven cities, the number of possible paths grows so quickly
>as the number of cities increases that larger problems are simply
>impossible. He said that 100-city problems are challenging and that if there
>were 1,000 cities in the path, no computer could at present solve the problem.
>
>   Adleman began by assigning each city a name in the form of a specific
>sequence of letters represented by chemical units of DNA. Each city had a first
>name that consisted of a string of 10 arbitrarily selected DNA letters and a
>last name that consisted of a different configuration of 10 DNA letters.
>
>   Translating the names into DNA letters, he said, is analogous to the first
>step an ordinary computer would take, which would be to translate the city
>names into strings of 0's and 1's. In a sense, it is using the language of DNA
>rather than the language of computers to label the cities.
>
>   Next Adleman gave each city a set of ``flight numbers.'' A flight number for
>a flight between Baltimore and Chicago, for example, would consist of the last
>name of Baltimore in the DNA code appended to the first name of Chicago in the
>DNA code, except that these sequences of DNA would be expressed in their mirror
>opposites of the sequences for the cities. (In the four-letter alphabet of DNA,
>A is the opposite of T and has a strong chemical affinity with it, and C is the
>opposite of G.)
>
>   Adleman then ordered the synthesis of DNA molecules corresponding to the
>cities and to the flight tags representing all the possible flights between one
>city and another.
>
>   Then he mixed all the DNA segments together. What happened then was that
>each flight tag started pairing with the DNA for its two cities, building up
>longer and longer chains of DNA representing flights between more and more
>cities.
>
>   As the DNA segments sloshed through the solution and bumped into one other,
>they almost instantaneously created all possible paths linking the seven
>cities.
>
>   Using well-established techniques of molecular biology, Adleman could fish
>out the molecule that started with the designated starting city, ended with the
>designated ending city, contained all the intermediate cities and was shortest
>in length. The sequence of this molecule represented the computed solution of
>the problem.
>
>   Adleman said he did not do the study to make a commercially applicable
>computing system, but instead to show that it is possible to exploit the
>computational power of biological systems.
>
>   ``All of this comes from the fact that if you hold up, say, a tablespoon of
>water, you have something like 10 to the 23rd power molecules. It's just a huge
>number. If you can orchestrate and choreograph them to do something all at
>once, you can execute operations thousands of times faster than the fastest
>supercomputers,'' he said.
>
>   ``The energy efficiency is just stunning,'' Adleman added. He explained that
>a joule, a unit of energy, is the amount of energy that is needed to burn a
>100-watt light bulb for one second. Thermodynamic theory, he says, predicts
>that one joule can fuel 34 times 10 to the 19th power operations.  A cell can
>perform 2 times 10 to the 19th power operations. But a supercomputer can only
>do 10 to the 10th power operations, making a cell 10 to the 10th power times
>more efficient.
>
>   The same goes for information storage, Adleman said. Cells can store one bit
>of information in one nanometer of space. A videotape stores one bit in 10 to
>the 12th power nanometers, taking a trillion times more space than is used in
>cells.
>
>   ``For me, the main point is not that DNA can compute so well, it's that it
>can compute at all,'' Adleman said.
>
>   When computer scientists start to view DNA as a computer, he said, ``we have
>a chance to look at a system of computation that's evolved for millions of
>years,'' adding, ``We might reasonably expect that it has evolved computational
>tricks that would be of great interest to a computer scientist.''
>
>   Gifford said that for him, it is these philosophical issues that make the
>work so intriguing.
>
>   ``Some might say, `Well, it really isn't practical,' '' Gifford said.  But,
>he added, the new finding, ``is like a stake in the ground,'' marking what he
>expects will be the start of a new era.
